    "durationMs": 260595,
    "finalText": "The URL Shortener API is complete and fully verified. I built it with ASP.NET Core 8 minimal APIs + EF Core 8/Npgsql (PostgreSQL), and every requirement passed an end-to-end curl test run against a server started with `sh start.sh`:\n\n- **POST /links** — 201 with `{\"code\",\"url\",\"clicks\":0}` (submitted URL returned unchanged, generated 8-char codes when omitted); duplicate custom code → 409; malformed JSON, missing/non-absolute/non-http(s) URL, and invalid codes (too short/long, spaces, non-ASCII, empty) all → 422 with an `error` field\n- **GET /links/{code}** — 200 or 404 with error; **GET /links** — `{\"items\":[...],\"total\":n}` in ascending creation order\n- **GET /r/{code}** — 302 with correct `Location`, clicks incremented exactly once per hit (verified 3 redirects → clicks 3), unknown code → 404\n- **Persistence** — restarted the server via start.sh and confirmed links and click counts survive; `dotnet ef database update` is idempotent\n\nImplementation notes: `DATABASE_URL` (a `postgresql://` URL) is parsed into an Npgsql connection string at runtime — no hard-coded credentials; migrations live in `Migrations/` (applied by start.sh, with a startup `Database.Migrate()` safety net); the `dotnet-ef` tool is pinned in `.config/dotnet-tools.json`; unique-code collisions are handled atomically via a unique index plus `ExecuteUpdateAsync` for race-free click counting. start.sh applies migrations (with a brief retry while Postgres starts) then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`, and the README documents all of it. The test server is stopped, so port 8000 is free for the grader."
  }
